WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:03.710
- Last year, Charli XCX
tweeted, "What is hyperpop?"

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:06.940
Comments ranged from full-blown
deconstructions of genre,

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:11.100
to, "It's just pop" to many
simply answering "You."

00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:13.650
- [Arthur] Though the
microgenre-turned-online scene

00:00:13.650 --> 00:00:16.810
has experienced many changes
over its short lifespan,

00:00:16.810 --> 00:00:19.640
there's no one way to define hyperpop.

00:00:19.640 --> 00:00:21.230
Ask anyone from the scene

00:00:21.230 --> 00:00:22.470
and they'll give you a different answer.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:24.290
Pop music is constantly evolving,

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:27.310
and hyperpop seems like
the next logical step.

00:00:27.310 --> 00:00:29.847
- In this video, we're going
to dig into the question,

00:00:29.847 --> 00:00:32.930
"What is hyperpop?" by
exploring its musical origin,

00:00:32.930 --> 00:00:33.790
and at the end,

00:00:33.790 --> 00:00:36.100
we're gonna make our own
hyperpop inspired track.

00:00:36.100 --> 00:00:37.568
So make sure to stick around.

00:00:37.568 --> 00:00:40.950
(upbeat pop music)

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:42.520
- Like most things on the Internet,

00:00:42.520 --> 00:00:45.230
hyperpop has evolved
rapidly over the years.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:47.230
Recently, its popularity has grown

00:00:47.230 --> 00:00:49.550
from playlists on Spotify and Apple Music,

00:00:49.550 --> 00:00:52.580
and many hyperpop tracks
have caught fire on TikTok.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.273
♪ Feel so clean like a money machine ♪

00:00:55.273 --> 00:00:57.481
♪ Feel so clean like a money machine ♪

00:00:57.481 --> 00:00:59.760
- [Linda] Going viral,
I feel like is something

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:02.350
that also is linked to hyperpop in a way.

00:01:02.350 --> 00:01:04.170
- Yeah. Oh yeah, definitely.

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.060
I feel like viral things

00:01:06.060 --> 00:01:07.980
are typically things that just like,

00:01:07.980 --> 00:01:09.530
people don't understand at first.

00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:11.522
That quest to understand these things,

00:01:11.522 --> 00:01:13.360
I think is like the funnest part about it.

00:01:13.360 --> 00:01:14.757
It's like understand
this music and be like,

00:01:14.757 --> 00:01:16.690
"All right, why did
someone ever make something

00:01:16.690 --> 00:01:18.158
that sounds like this?
I hate it." And then

00:01:18.158 --> 00:01:18.991
all of a sudden you're like...
- [Linda] Yeah.

00:01:18.991 --> 00:01:20.530
- ... Wait this actually
slaps really hard.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:21.730
- Yeah. I'm thinking about this.

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:23.540
I'm listening to this in the shower.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:25.160
Why is this so catchy?

00:01:25.160 --> 00:01:27.970
That's what happened to me
then I got TikTok recently

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:28.910
and I was like, whoa,

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:31.110
this is like the sound of a generation.

00:01:31.110 --> 00:01:33.060
- So where did hyperpop come from?

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:36.820
Hyperpop's origins can be
traced back to the mid 2000s

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:39.910
with the rise of the
Nightcore scene on YouTube.

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:42.270
Nightcore is a genre that revolves around

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.400
increasing the pitch and
tempo of pre-existing songs

00:01:45.400 --> 00:01:48.749
and is often paired with
anime visuals or cover art.

00:01:48.749 --> 00:01:50.020
♪ Cause every time we touch ♪

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.306
♪ I feel this static ♪

00:01:51.306 --> 00:01:52.376
♪ And every time we kiss ♪

00:01:52.376 --> 00:01:53.910
♪ I reach for the sky ♪

00:01:53.910 --> 00:01:56.510
- [Linda] According to Spotify
analyst, Glenn McDonald,

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.560
the term hyperpop started being used

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.060
in reference to the UK based,
PC Music collective in 2014,

00:02:03.060 --> 00:02:05.190
which was founded by producer A.G Cook.

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:07.120
Just like many other new genres,

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.840
many critics did not respond
well to the fresh sound.

00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:12.360
In the 2010s, PC Music and it's artists

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:13.820
such as Hannah Diamond,

00:02:13.820 --> 00:02:15.240
Danny L. Harle,

00:02:15.240 --> 00:02:17.450
Girlfriend Of The Year and Kane West

00:02:17.450 --> 00:02:19.100
created innovative pop music

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:21.290
defined by its blend of perky melodies

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:23.676
and frenetic digital production.

00:02:23.676 --> 00:02:24.776
(upbeat music)

00:02:24.776 --> 00:02:27.141
♪ Beautiful ♪

00:02:27.141 --> 00:02:28.380
♪ Baby when you look at me ♪

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:29.750
♪ You know that I'll be here forever ♪

00:02:29.750 --> 00:02:32.090
- I think having really
heavy hitting drum samples

00:02:32.090 --> 00:02:34.750
is really important and really like,

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:37.680
focusing on the sound design,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:40.670
you know, having this
sort of like crystalline,

00:02:40.670 --> 00:02:43.310
like it's sort of this balance
between these hyper like

00:02:43.310 --> 00:02:46.410
shiny sounds and these super
distorted, messed up sounds.

00:02:46.410 --> 00:02:50.090
- Do you feel like that
distortion is what differentiates

00:02:50.090 --> 00:02:52.300
hyperpop from regular pop?

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:53.250
- I think that's definitely

00:02:53.250 --> 00:02:54.670
one of the clearest things, I think.

00:02:54.670 --> 00:02:57.240
From a technical
standpoint from, you know,

00:02:57.240 --> 00:03:00.000
mixing and mastering,
like when I'm mastering

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:01.140
a hyperpop song,

00:03:01.140 --> 00:03:03.400
I'm taking the limiter and I'm smashing it

00:03:03.400 --> 00:03:05.000
because that's the sound.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:07.583
(upbeat music)

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:10.570
- Many of the newer figures of hyperpop

00:03:10.570 --> 00:03:13.010
have taken the exaggerated
eclectic elements,

00:03:13.010 --> 00:03:16.710
laid down by PC music, and
combined them with hip hop,

00:03:16.710 --> 00:03:18.628
like the sounds of cloud rap.

00:03:18.628 --> 00:03:20.610
- SoundCloud has played a monumental role

00:03:20.610 --> 00:03:22.750
in the birth and growth of hyperpop.

00:03:22.750 --> 00:03:24.800
And it's given smaller hyperpop artists,

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:26.720
a place to share their
music with the world.

00:03:26.720 --> 00:03:29.712
Artists Osquinn,
previously known as P4RKR,

00:03:29.712 --> 00:03:30.690
SEBII

00:03:30.690 --> 00:03:31.770
and Saturn,

00:03:31.770 --> 00:03:34.860
are influenced by mid
2010's SoundCloud emo rap,

00:03:34.860 --> 00:03:36.230
like Yung Lean

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:39.480
and more mainstream
artists like Playboi Carti.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:41.530
Artists like DOLLYWOOD1,

00:03:41.530 --> 00:03:43.920
BLACKWINTERWELLS and KID TRASH

00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:46.910
are more focused on
heavily distorted 808s,

00:03:46.910 --> 00:03:49.910
playful melodies and unnatural auto-tune,

00:03:49.910 --> 00:03:52.737
creating a blend of rap, pop and EDM.

00:03:52.737 --> 00:03:53.661
(mellow hyperpop music)

00:03:53.661 --> 00:03:57.182
♪ I sit back and they all
look at me and laugh ♪

00:03:57.182 --> 00:03:58.870
♪ That's not how I began ♪

00:03:58.870 --> 00:04:00.900
- [Linda] Probably the
most influential figure

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:04.380
in the hyperpop scene is
Scottish producer, Sophie.

00:04:04.380 --> 00:04:07.320
- She's in everything, all of
these songs that exist now,

00:04:07.320 --> 00:04:10.293
there's like some piece
of her that is within it,

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:13.943
in this kind of like almost
like spiritual way for me.

00:04:14.828 --> 00:04:16.350
And I think, yeah, Arca, you know,

00:04:16.350 --> 00:04:19.290
it was always like queer and trans artists

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.430
who were at the forefront.

00:04:20.430 --> 00:04:21.840
- Sophie was obsessed

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.980
with the way a bubble has an
ascending pitch when popped.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.130
You can hear Sophie play
around with this sound

00:04:27.130 --> 00:04:29.201
on tracks like "Lemonade".

00:04:29.201 --> 00:04:32.459
♪ Candy boys c-c-candy boys ♪

00:04:32.459 --> 00:04:33.960
♪ Lemonade l-l-lemonade ♪

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:35.380
- [Arthur] Sophie's debut album,

00:04:35.380 --> 00:04:37.810
the "Oil of Every Pearl's Un-Insides"

00:04:37.810 --> 00:04:41.090
received high acclaim for its
continued evolution of sound.

00:04:41.090 --> 00:04:42.550
The album was even nominated

00:04:42.550 --> 00:04:46.860
for the best dance/electronic
album at the 2019 Grammy's.

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:51.090
Sophie tragically passed
away January, 2021

00:04:51.090 --> 00:04:54.650
at the age of 34 after an accidental fall.

00:04:54.650 --> 00:04:56.060
After the news of the accident

00:04:56.060 --> 00:04:57.920
reached the hyperpop community,

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:01.160
many artists came together on
social media to mourn the loss

00:05:01.160 --> 00:05:03.123
of one of their greatest influences.

00:05:04.120 --> 00:05:05.914
- [Linda] While hyperpop was brewing,

00:05:05.914 --> 00:05:09.230
Charli XCX was already
a rising pop figure,

00:05:09.230 --> 00:05:11.240
looking to evolve her sound.

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:14.440
Charli rose to prominence at
the UK warehouse rave scene

00:05:14.440 --> 00:05:17.610
and admired artists such
as Justice and Uffie.

00:05:17.610 --> 00:05:21.330
Now she wanted to create music
that reflected her roots.

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:25.190
After penning an email to
producer Sophie in 2015,

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:30.190
they began collaborating on
her 2016 EP, "Vroom Vroom".

00:05:30.241 --> 00:05:31.846
♪ Let me ride let me ride ♪

00:05:31.846 --> 00:05:35.370
♪ All my life I've been
waitin' for a good time ♪

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:38.000
- When Charli first
released "Vroom Vroom",

00:05:38.000 --> 00:05:40.110
it was met with a lot of confusion

00:05:40.110 --> 00:05:42.370
and critics were quick to write it off.

00:05:42.370 --> 00:05:46.460
In 2016, A.G. Cook became
Charli's creative director.

00:05:46.460 --> 00:05:49.640
He would go on to produce
her next four projects.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:51.080
Number One Angel,

00:05:51.080 --> 00:05:51.913
Pop 2,

00:05:51.913 --> 00:05:55.050
Charli and How I'm Feeling Now.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.610
Pop 2 is considered by many to
be the blueprint of hyperpop

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:01.501
and everything that PC music
has been leading up to.

00:06:01.501 --> 00:06:02.509
♪ Can you unlock it ♪

00:06:02.509 --> 00:06:06.795
♪ Caught in the liplock when we pitstop ♪

00:06:06.795 --> 00:06:08.750
- Hyperpop hit mainstream awareness

00:06:08.750 --> 00:06:11.350
with the creation of the
hyperpop Spotify playlist

00:06:11.350 --> 00:06:12.780
in August, 2019.

00:06:12.780 --> 00:06:15.280
- [Linda] Lizzy Szabo,
the playlist main curator,

00:06:15.280 --> 00:06:18.530
created the playlist in response
to the unexpected success

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:21.850
of a 100 Gecs' debut album, 1,000 Gecs.

00:06:21.850 --> 00:06:23.700
- They had tested some
of the group's songs

00:06:23.700 --> 00:06:26.170
on different playlists,
but felt their unique sound

00:06:26.170 --> 00:06:27.750
needed its own place

00:06:27.750 --> 00:06:30.580
and so came the birth of
the hyperpop playlist.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:32.727
- If it wasn't for Spotify playlist

00:06:32.727 --> 00:06:36.280
and the articles that were
coming out at the time,

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:37.360
the community of friends

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.570
and the people I was making music with,

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:43.040
slowly bit by bit, started
getting recognition

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:45.870
by one artist, by another,
by another, by another.

00:06:45.870 --> 00:06:49.210
They generally started to build
a following for themselves,

00:06:49.210 --> 00:06:52.630
but a lot of people used
hyperpop, not even used,

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:53.730
but a lot of people were...

00:06:53.730 --> 00:06:55.070
That label really was thrown on us.

00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:57.190
We really was just making
music like SoundCloud kids

00:06:57.190 --> 00:06:58.660
making music from our bedrooms.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:00.130
And then afterwards it grew

00:07:00.130 --> 00:07:02.470
and bloomed into
something even bigger than

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:04.530
I think any of us ever expected it to be.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.100
- [Linda] The playlist was a hit

00:07:06.100 --> 00:07:10.010
and now has over 240,000 subscribers,

00:07:10.010 --> 00:07:13.480
making it one of the most
popular on the entire platform.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:16.210
A few months later, controversy struck

00:07:16.210 --> 00:07:19.400
when A. G. Cook did his own
takeover of the playlist

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:21.250
and added different artists,

00:07:21.250 --> 00:07:23.130
including Iggy Azalea,

00:07:23.130 --> 00:07:27.470
Madonna, Vince Staples,
Nicki Minaj and Kate Bush,

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:31.150
all of whom don't exactly fit
under the hyperpop umbrella.

00:07:31.150 --> 00:07:34.430
Not only did his choices bump
many smaller artists of color

00:07:34.430 --> 00:07:35.840
off the playlist,

00:07:35.840 --> 00:07:37.480
but it caused lots of confusion

00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:40.200
around what exactly constitutes hyperpop.

00:07:40.200 --> 00:07:43.000
Cook later defended his
choices in a Twitter statement,

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:44.750
and claimed he didn't know these artists

00:07:44.750 --> 00:07:46.870
would be taken off of the playlist.

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.220
He then issued a revised
version of the playlist

00:07:49.220 --> 00:07:51.270
that included more underground acts.

00:07:51.270 --> 00:07:54.270
To many, hyperpop is
more than just a genre

00:07:54.270 --> 00:07:57.350
but an online centered
community and movement.

00:07:57.350 --> 00:08:00.260
- Yeah, I think hyperpop is sort of like

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.610
an umbrella for a ton
of smaller communities.

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:07.610
Like I feel like I'm part of like several

00:08:07.610 --> 00:08:10.920
very like niche, small
communities that all kind of exist

00:08:10.920 --> 00:08:12.430
under the world of hyperpop.

00:08:12.430 --> 00:08:15.150
- [Arthur] In 2021, the
term Digicore came about

00:08:15.150 --> 00:08:17.120
after emerging from SoundCloud.

00:08:17.120 --> 00:08:21.100
Unlike its ancestors of PC
music, which is mainly white,

00:08:21.100 --> 00:08:23.810
Digicore is made up of
mostly queer people of color.

00:08:23.810 --> 00:08:26.220
- Hyperpop kind of got thrown on us when

00:08:26.220 --> 00:08:28.420
a lot of the more major
artists and bigger companies

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:29.480
started to see

00:08:30.560 --> 00:08:32.440
just everything like

00:08:32.440 --> 00:08:34.805
start to shoot up.
- This whole world...

00:08:34.805 --> 00:08:36.409
... of Soundcloud musicians,

00:08:36.409 --> 00:08:37.242
right.
- [Midwxst] Yeah,

00:08:37.242 --> 00:08:38.075
it was starting to be discovered.

00:08:38.075 --> 00:08:39.950
But Digicore is the one that
a lot of people went with

00:08:39.950 --> 00:08:42.530
because it was like, a lot
of songs that people make

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:45.668
in the hyperpop scene aren't pop at all.

00:08:45.668 --> 00:08:47.630
It's hyper-electronic.
- [Arthur] Yeah.

00:08:47.630 --> 00:08:49.940
- It's hyper-ambient,
it's hyper drum and bass.

00:08:49.940 --> 00:08:52.630
- [Arthur] Yeah.
- It's never really pop.

00:08:52.630 --> 00:08:55.490
(upbeat music)

00:08:55.490 --> 00:08:56.670
- [Arthur] When I think of hyperpop,

00:08:56.670 --> 00:09:00.860
I'm thinking of a lot of stuff
going on at the same time,

00:09:00.860 --> 00:09:04.927
I'm thinking of simple melodies
occurring at the same time.

00:09:04.927 --> 00:09:07.960
(upbeat hyperpop music)

00:09:07.960 --> 00:09:09.430
- Just listening to hyperpop,

00:09:09.430 --> 00:09:12.860
I could see all of the sub genres

00:09:12.860 --> 00:09:15.720
that go under that huge
umbrella of hyperpop.

00:09:15.720 --> 00:09:19.760
- I don't know if this is gonna be right,

00:09:19.760 --> 00:09:22.472
but I feel like it's not wrong, you know?

00:09:22.472 --> 00:09:24.480
- I think that's like the
whole hyperpop attitude

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:25.313
is just like,

00:09:25.313 --> 00:09:26.340
we're not really aiming for what's right,

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.260
we're just aiming for what feels good.

00:09:28.260 --> 00:09:29.093
There ain't no wrong answers.
- Yeah.

00:09:29.093 --> 00:09:31.820
- That's what really
I'm discovering is like,

00:09:31.820 --> 00:09:34.021
there are no wrong answers.

00:09:34.021 --> 00:09:35.053
(electronic beat)

00:09:35.053 --> 00:09:35.886
- There, go.

00:09:35.886 --> 00:09:36.719
(electronic beat)

00:09:36.719 --> 00:09:38.074
- There, go.

00:09:38.074 --> 00:09:40.657
(upbeat music)

00:09:43.090 --> 00:09:45.860
- What you did with the vocals...

00:09:45.860 --> 00:09:49.040
(upbeat music)

00:09:49.040 --> 00:09:52.489
- The sentiment behind
the lyrics too like...

00:09:52.489 --> 00:09:54.471
(Arthur singing)

00:09:54.471 --> 00:09:55.304
- Yeah.

00:09:55.304 --> 00:09:56.582
- It's like it has some spice to it.

00:09:56.582 --> 00:09:59.384
♪ Got me thinking I don't
like your attitude ♪

00:09:59.384 --> 00:10:04.384
♪ Got me thinking I don't
like your attitude ♪

00:10:05.140 --> 00:10:09.660
- I was admiring the way that
these hyperpop artists are

00:10:09.660 --> 00:10:14.588
using like production as a
way to express their emotions

00:10:14.588 --> 00:10:15.900
when they're songwriting.

00:10:15.900 --> 00:10:17.884
Like I just thought it was really cool.

00:10:17.884 --> 00:10:21.092
♪ Got me thinking I don't
like your attitude ♪

00:10:21.092 --> 00:10:24.101
(upbeat music)

00:10:24.101 --> 00:10:28.077
♪ Got me thinking I don't
like your attitude ♪

00:10:28.077 --> 00:10:29.327
- That's crazy.

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:32.130
One more thing,

00:10:32.130 --> 00:10:35.010
we want to invite you to
take the PBS Digital Studios,

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:36.850
annual audience survey.

00:10:36.850 --> 00:10:39.230
It helps us a lot if Sound
Field fans participate,

00:10:39.230 --> 00:10:41.310
you even get to vote
on potential new shows.

00:10:41.310 --> 00:10:43.290
There's a link in the description below

00:10:43.290 --> 00:10:46.160
and if you have a few minutes,
we will love your input.

00:10:46.160 --> 00:10:46.993
Thanks.

